    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          In a three-day outage the question is not whether the generator can run continuously, because it cannot. It is how you structure the stops so that the fridge stays cold and the engine survives to the next storm.
        </p>

        <div id="two-limits" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Two Different Limits, and People Only Know About One
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ask how long a generator can run and most answers quote the fuel tank, because that is the specification printed on the box. It is a real limit and it is the less important one.
          </p>

          <p>
            The fuel limit is straightforward: tank capacity divided by consumption at your load. Most consumer portables land between five and twelve hours at half load, and inverter generators in eco mode can stretch further because they throttle the engine down when demand falls.
          </p>

          <p>
            The mechanical limit is oil, heat and wear, and it is the one that decides what you can actually do in a long outage. A small air-cooled engine running at load is working hard. It consumes some oil, it gets hot, and it has no cooling system beyond a fan and the air moving past it. Manufacturers do not design these engines to run indefinitely, and their maintenance intervals reflect that.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical consequence is that a generator with a twelve-hour tank cannot simply be refuelled twelve times to run for six days. Somewhere in there it needs to stop, cool, and have its oil checked and probably changed. Planning for that in advance is the difference between a generator that survives the outage and one that seizes on day three.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE HEADLINE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Fuel measures hours. Oil measures whether you still have a generator afterwards.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A typical air-cooled portable is fine running 8 to 12 hours at a stretch, wants a shutdown and an oil check around the 24-hour mark, and needs an oil change far sooner in continuous service than the calendar interval suggests. Plan the stops rather than discovering them.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="runtime-tank" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Runtime by Tank Size and Load
          </h2>

          <p>
            Fuel consumption scales with load, not with generator size, which is why the quoted runtime is always given at a stated percentage.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Generator Class</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical Tank</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Runtime at 25%</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Runtime at 50%</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Runtime at Full</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">2,000 W inverter</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1.0-1.2 gal</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">10-12 hr</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">6-8 hr</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3-4 hr</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">3,500 W inverter</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2.0-2.5 gal</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">12-16 hr</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">8-11 hr</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4-6 hr</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">5,000 W portable</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4-5 gal</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">14-18 hr</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">9-12 hr</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5-7 hr</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">7,500 W portable</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">6-8 gal</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">12-16 hr</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">8-11 hr</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5-6 hr</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Propane, 20 lb tank</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4.7 gal LP</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">10-14 hr</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">6-9 hr</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4-5 hr</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Two things are worth noticing. Runtime at quarter load is often double the full-load figure, which is a strong argument for not oversizing your generator and for managing what you run at once. And the manufacturer's headline runtime is almost always quoted at 25 percent load, which is a lighter draw than most people actually impose.
          </p>

          <p>
            Inverter generators do better than the table suggests in real use because of eco or economy mode, which lets the engine drop to idle speed when demand falls rather than holding a fixed 3,600 rpm. Over a night where the only load is a fridge cycling on and off, that can add several hours and a great deal of quiet.
          </p>

          <p>
            Propane runs slightly less energy-dense than gasoline, so you lose perhaps ten percent of runtime for the same tank volume. What you gain is fuel that stores indefinitely without stabiliser and, crucially for this topic, the ability to swap tanks without shutting down, which the refuelling section covers.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want to work out your own numbers rather than use a table, our generator wattage chart lists typical appliance draws so you can estimate your actual load, and our guide on how many watts it takes to power a house puts that into context.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="oil" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Oil Is the Real Ceiling
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the section that matters if you take one thing from the page.
          </p>

          <p>
            Small air-cooled engines consume oil in normal operation, and they consume more of it working hard and hot. A generator carrying a real load for twenty-four hours can drop its oil level noticeably, and running one low on oil is how engines are destroyed. Many modern units have a low-oil shutdown sensor, which is a genuine safeguard and is not a substitute for checking, because the sensor protects the engine by stopping it — which in an outage means your fridge quietly warming up at three in the morning.
          </p>

          <p>
            Oil also degrades far faster in continuous hot service than the calendar suggests. Manufacturers typically specify an oil change interval in running hours, commonly somewhere between 50 and 200 depending on the engine, with a much shorter first change during break-in, often at 20 to 30 hours. In a multi-day outage you can burn through a full interval in two or three days, which is why an oil change during a long outage is a normal operation rather than an emergency.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical routine is straightforward. Check the oil every time you refuel, which happens naturally because the machine is already shut down and cooling. Top up as needed with the correct grade. And have enough oil and a filter, if your engine takes one, on hand before the outage rather than discovering the hardware store is shut.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our guide on how often to change generator oil covers the grades and intervals in detail, including the distinction between the break-in change and the routine one. The short version for storm season: know your interval in hours, keep a log, and store two changes' worth of oil with the generator.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Oil Numbers</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">20-30 hr</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical break-in oil change</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">50-200 hr</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Routine interval, engine dependent</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">Every refuel</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">How often to check the level</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">2 changes</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Oil to keep on hand for storm season</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="manufacturers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Manufacturers Actually Specify
          </h2>

          <p>
            Guidance varies by engine class, and knowing which class you own sets realistic expectations.
          </p>

          <p>
            Consumer air-cooled portables, which is most of what people own, are generally happy running 8 to 12 hours at a stretch and are commonly advised to be shut down and allowed to cool at least once in every 24 hours. That stop is for the oil check and to let the engine come down to ambient, not because it is about to fail at hour 25.
          </p>

          <p>
            Inverter generators are similar mechanically but often gentler in practice because eco mode reduces the average engine speed substantially over a mixed load. They are still air-cooled small engines with the same oil considerations.
          </p>

          <p>
            Liquid-cooled standby generators are a different category entirely. Designed for extended operation, with a proper cooling system and often an automatic exercise cycle, they can run for days or weeks with periodic maintenance. If your expectation is continuous power through a week-long outage without stops, that is the equipment class that delivers it, and our comparison of portable and standby generators covers the trade.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Class</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Comfortable Stretch</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Shutdown Interval</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Extended Outage Suitability</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Air-cooled portable</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">8-12 hr</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Every 24 hr</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Workable with planned stops</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Inverter portable</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">8-14 hr</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Every 24 hr</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Workable, quieter, thriftier</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Air-cooled standby</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24 hr+</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Per maintenance schedule</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Good</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Liquid-cooled standby</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Days to weeks</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Per maintenance schedule</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Designed for it</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Your manual is the authority for your machine, and it is worth reading the maintenance section before storm season rather than during one. Note the oil interval in hours, note whether there is an air filter service interval, and note the manufacturer's guidance on continuous operation, because it varies more than people assume.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="refuelling" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Refuelling Without Shutting Down, and Why You Mostly Cannot
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the question behind the question, because what people usually want is uninterrupted power rather than uninterrupted engine operation.
          </p>

          <p>
            Never refuel a running gasoline generator, and never refuel a hot one. Petrol spilled onto a hot exhaust or muffler ignites, and generator fires during refuelling are a well-documented way people are badly burned. Shut it down, let it cool for several minutes, then refuel. This is not excessive caution; it is the single most common cause of generator fires.
          </p>

          <p>
            Propane changes the picture entirely, and this is its underrated advantage. A dual-fuel or tri-fuel generator running on propane can have a second tank connected through a changeover regulator, or a larger tank fitted, so fuel supply continues without stopping the engine. There is no open fuel handling near a hot engine at all. Our dual-fuel generator guide covers the machines that offer this.
          </p>

          <p>
            Natural gas, on a tri-fuel unit plumbed to the house supply, removes the fuel limit altogether. As long as the gas supply holds, the constraint becomes purely mechanical: oil and heat. For anyone in an area with frequent multi-day outages and a gas connection, this is the arrangement that makes extended running practical.
          </p>

          <p>
            Extended run fuel tanks exist for gasoline units, essentially an auxiliary tank feeding the generator's own. They work, they lengthen the interval between stops considerably, and they need to be set up correctly with attention to fuel line routing and vapour. They do not remove the oil constraint.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever the fuel, plan the stops around your fridge and freezer. A full freezer holds safely for around 48 hours unopened and a fridge for around four hours, so a shutdown of fifteen minutes for refuelling and an oil check costs you nothing as long as the doors stay shut.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="heat-wear" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Heat, Duty Cycle and What Actually Wears Out
          </h2>

          <p>
            Beyond oil, three things accumulate damage during long runs, and all three are manageable.
          </p>

          <p>
            Heat is the main one. An air-cooled engine sheds heat through fins and a fan, and that only works if air can move freely around the machine. A generator tucked against a wall, under a tight cover, or boxed in by a makeshift shelter runs hotter than designed. Give it clear space on all sides, keep the air intake unobstructed, and if you are covering it against rain use a purpose-made running cover rather than a tarpaulin. Our guide on running a generator in the rain covers what is safe.
          </p>

          <p>
            Load matters more than people think. Running continuously at or near full rated output is hard on an engine and on the alternator, and rated output is generally a peak rather than a continuous figure — many generators quote both a running and a starting watts number for exactly this reason. Sitting at 50 to 75 percent of running watts is comfortable; sitting at 100 percent for days is not.
          </p>

          <p>
            Very light loading has its own problem on conventional generators. An engine held at 3,600 rpm with almost nothing connected can run rich, glaze cylinder bores and build carbon, a phenomenon usually called wet stacking on larger diesels but with a small-engine equivalent. Inverter generators avoid this by throttling down. If you have a conventional unit and a tiny load, it is better to consolidate loads onto it than to run it near-empty for days.
          </p>

          <p>
            The air filter is the maintenance item that gets forgotten during an outage. A generator running for days outdoors, particularly in dusty or post-storm conditions, loads its filter faster than normal, and a choked filter makes the engine run rich and hot. Check it when you check the oil.
          </p>

          <p>
            None of this means a portable generator is fragile. It means it is a small engine doing a demanding job, and treating it like the lawnmower engine it is related to — regular oil, clean air, sensible load, room to breathe — is what gets it through a week.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="outage-plan" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. A Realistic Multi-Day Outage Plan
          </h2>

          <p>
            What this looks like in practice over a three-day power cut.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not run it continuously, and this is the biggest practical realisation. Most households do not need power around the clock. Running the generator in blocks — a few hours in the morning to bring the fridge and freezer back down, a few hours in the evening for cooking, lights and device charging — cuts fuel consumption dramatically, extends the oil interval, and gives the engine cooling periods it would not otherwise get.
          </p>

          <p>
            A freezer that is full and stays shut holds for roughly 48 hours; a fridge holds around four. So a pattern of six to eight hours on, then off, keeps food safe comfortably while using perhaps a third of the fuel of continuous running. It also means the generator is silent overnight, which matters more than people expect on day three.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check oil at every refuel, without exception. It takes a minute, the machine is already off and cooling, and it is the check that prevents the expensive failure. Top up as needed and note the running hours.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plan an oil change if the outage runs long. If your interval is 50 hours and you are running eight hours a day, you reach it on day six. Having the oil, a drain pan and a funnel ready means that is a twenty-minute job rather than a problem.
          </p>

          <p>
            Store fuel safely and sensibly. Keep it in approved containers, well away from the generator and the house, and use fresh fuel with stabiliser. Gasoline degrades in months, which is why propane suits people who want a generator ready without a fuel rotation routine. Our guide on storing a portable generator covers off-season preparation.
          </p>

          <p>
            And keep the CO alarms working. The risk that actually kills people during outages is not engine wear, it is carbon monoxide from a generator run too close to the house or in a garage. Outdoors only, well away from doors, windows and vents, every single time. Our guide on where to place a generator outside covers the distances.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Planning Around Tank Size and Ignoring Oil</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Fuel sets the interval between stops; oil sets whether you still have a working engine afterwards. Small air-cooled engines consume oil under load and degrade it fast in continuous hot service, and a full interval can be burned through in two or three days of an outage. Check the level at every refuel.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Refuelling a Hot or Running Generator</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Petrol spilled onto a hot muffler ignites, and this is the single most common cause of generator fires and burns during outages. Shut it down and let it cool for several minutes before adding fuel. If you want genuinely uninterrupted supply, that is an argument for propane or natural gas, not for pouring fuel into a running machine.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running Continuously When You Do Not Need To</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most households do not need round-the-clock power. A full freezer holds about 48 hours unopened and a fridge about four, so running in six to eight hour blocks keeps food safe on roughly a third of the fuel, extends the oil interval, gives the engine cooling periods, and buys you a silent night.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Boxing the Generator In for Weather Protection</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An air-cooled engine sheds heat through fins and a fan, which needs free air movement on every side. A tarpaulin, a tight shelter or a machine pushed against a wall all raise running temperature well beyond design. Use a purpose-made running cover and leave clear space around it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running a Conventional Generator at Near-Zero Load for Days</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An engine held at 3,600 rpm with almost nothing connected runs rich, glazes bores and builds carbon. Inverter generators avoid this by throttling down, but a conventional unit is better consolidated onto a reasonable load than left idling near-empty for days on end.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long can a generator run continuously?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Fuel typically limits a portable to 8 to 12 hours at half load, and most manufacturers advise shutting down and letting the engine cool at least once every 24 hours so the oil can be checked. Liquid-cooled standby generators are a different class and are designed to run for days or weeks with periodic maintenance.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I run a generator 24 hours a day?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                You can run through a 24-hour period, but plan a shutdown within it for cooling and an oil check rather than treating it as a continuous machine. In a multi-day outage, running in six to eight hour blocks keeps food safe on far less fuel, extends the oil interval and gives the engine cooling time.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to change the oil during a long outage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Quite possibly. Intervals are specified in running hours, commonly 50 to 200 depending on engine, with a much shorter first change at 20 to 30 hours during break-in. Running eight hours a day reaches a 50-hour interval within a week. Keep two changes' worth of oil and a drain pan with the generator.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you refuel a generator while it's running?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not a gasoline one, ever. Petrol on a hot muffler ignites and this is a leading cause of generator fires. Shut down and let it cool for several minutes. Propane and natural gas units are different: a changeover regulator or a house gas connection lets fuel continue without stopping the engine, with no open fuel handling.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long will a generator run on a 20 lb propane tank?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Roughly 6 to 9 hours at half load on a typical portable, and 10 to 14 at quarter load. Propane is slightly less energy dense than gasoline so you lose around ten percent of runtime for the same volume, but it stores indefinitely without stabiliser and tanks can be swapped without shutting the engine down.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it bad to run a generator at full load continuously?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, over extended periods. Rated output is generally a peak rather than a continuous figure, which is why generators quote separate starting and running watts. Sitting at 50 to 75 percent of running watts is comfortable for an engine; holding 100 percent for days accelerates wear on both the engine and the alternator.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What happens if a generator runs out of oil?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most modern units have a low-oil shutdown that stops the engine to protect it, which saves the generator but means your power disappears without warning, quite possibly overnight. Without that sensor, running low destroys the engine. Either way, checking the level at every refuel is the habit that prevents it.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

