        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            A typical air-cooled portable will run 8 to 12 hours on a tank at half load, and should be shut down and allowed to cool at least once in every 24 hours so you can check the oil. That stop is not a failure point approaching, it is routine maintenance on a small engine doing hard work, and skipping it is how engines get destroyed in outages.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The better plan for a multi-day outage is not continuous running at all. Six to eight hour blocks keep a full freezer and a fridge safe on roughly a third of the fuel, extend the oil interval, give the engine cooling periods, and leave you a quiet night. Check the oil at every refuel, never add fuel to a hot machine, and keep it well away from the house with the CO alarms working.
          </p>
        </div>

